WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:05.120
What if I told you that, um, basically every single thing you value your ambition,

00:05.120 --> 00:08.960
your deepest love for your family, your desire to be a good person.

00:08.960 --> 00:13.200
Or even just, you know, your appreciation for a beautiful sunset is functionally

00:13.200 --> 00:16.080
no different than a beaver's blind instinct to build a dam.

00:16.080 --> 00:19.280
Oh, wow. Yeah, that is, that's heavy right out of the gate.

00:19.280 --> 00:22.880
Right. That there is absolutely no cosmic weight to any of it. None.

00:22.880 --> 00:25.600
It's a completely terrifying proposition, honestly.

00:25.600 --> 00:26.080
Yeah.

00:26.080 --> 00:30.080
Because we spend our entire lives convinced that the concrete we're standing on,

00:30.880 --> 00:33.280
you know, our morals, our loves, our goals.

00:33.280 --> 00:36.480
We think that concrete was poured by the universe itself.

00:36.480 --> 00:38.560
We assume there's a master blueprint somewhere.

00:38.560 --> 00:39.680
Yeah. We really do.

00:39.680 --> 00:42.480
And welcome to the deep dive, by the way.

00:42.480 --> 00:47.280
Today for you listening, we are basically taking a sledgehammer to that exact foundation,

00:47.280 --> 00:49.040
a very methodical sledgehammer.

00:49.040 --> 00:54.240
Exactly. We're exploring a profoundly reflective and honestly deeply unnerving essay by

00:54.320 --> 00:59.120
Simgi Yun. It's titled Coming Back to the Human A-Sequel to Why Do We Live.

00:59.120 --> 01:02.640
And I'll be perfectly honest with you, getting through this material requires a bit of

01:03.520 --> 01:05.040
philosophical stamina.

01:05.040 --> 01:06.640
That's a good way to put it, stamina.

01:07.280 --> 01:10.640
You have to be willing to look at the things you hold absolutely sacred

01:11.200 --> 01:13.680
and just watch them dissolve under a microscope.

01:13.680 --> 01:15.280
Which is necessary, right?

01:15.280 --> 01:18.640
Because of the premise the author established in their previous work,

01:18.640 --> 01:22.320
we kind of have to start there to understand the stakes of this essay.

01:22.400 --> 01:26.160
Right. The original argument was that the meaning of life isn't something you discover.

01:26.160 --> 01:30.000
Like you don't just wander around the desert waiting to unearth a glowing box of meaning.

01:30.000 --> 01:31.760
Like it's buried treasure or something.

01:31.760 --> 01:34.960
Exactly. It's not a treasure. It's something you actively create.

01:34.960 --> 01:38.560
Which, you know, sounds super liberating at first glance.

01:38.560 --> 01:41.600
It shifts the burden off the universe and onto you.

01:41.600 --> 01:44.960
You don't have to beg the cosmos for your purpose. You just build it yourself.

01:44.960 --> 01:50.400
But taking that idea seriously, like really seriously opens up a pretty massive sinkhole.

01:50.400 --> 01:55.600
Yeah, a terrifying one. Because if the universe isn't handing out meaning,

01:55.600 --> 01:57.120
and it's entirely up to us,

01:58.160 --> 02:00.160
what actually deserves to be honored.

02:00.160 --> 02:02.400
That is the ultimate dilemma right there.

02:02.400 --> 02:04.880
If you are the sole architect of your values,

02:04.880 --> 02:09.200
and there is no like universal blueprint to check your work against,

02:09.200 --> 02:10.640
what justifies anything?

02:10.640 --> 02:13.120
Why build a hospital instead of a casino, right?

02:13.120 --> 02:16.320
Exactly. Why pour your heart into raising children instead of just

02:16.320 --> 02:18.560
staring blankly at a wall for 80 years?

02:18.560 --> 02:20.560
Logically, it's one actually better than the other.

02:21.120 --> 02:25.360
So today, we're going to use cold razor sharp logic to dismantle all of it.

02:25.360 --> 02:28.000
We're going to strip away the romance of human connection,

02:28.000 --> 02:30.480
tear down the whole concept of human greatness,

02:30.480 --> 02:34.080
and just look directly into the absolute void of meaninglessness.

02:34.080 --> 02:35.120
It sounds bleak, I know.

02:35.760 --> 02:38.800
But, and this is why this deep dive is so vital,

02:38.800 --> 02:40.400
we aren't staying in the void.

02:40.400 --> 02:45.120
We're going to find a wildly unexpected, deeply profound reason to care about

02:45.120 --> 02:46.800
this messy human experience anyway.

02:47.280 --> 02:49.520
We just have to survive the dismantling first.

02:49.520 --> 02:52.160
And before we even pick up the tools to do that,

02:52.160 --> 02:55.040
the author hits us with a massive structural roadblock.

02:55.040 --> 02:56.400
Yeah, the meta problem.

02:56.400 --> 02:56.720
Right.

02:57.520 --> 03:00.160
The very tool we are using right now,

03:00.160 --> 03:02.960
the English language, the actual words forming in your mind,

03:02.960 --> 03:04.080
as you listen to us,

03:04.080 --> 03:09.440
might be fundamentally incapable of grasping the truth we're hunting for here.

03:09.440 --> 03:11.520
The language trap, I love this part,

03:11.520 --> 03:14.480
because it's like trying to catch water with a fishnet.

03:14.480 --> 03:16.800
You can see the water, you can describe the water,

03:16.800 --> 03:19.040
but the net itself just can't hold it.

03:19.040 --> 03:21.360
The author grounds this whole problem

03:21.360 --> 03:23.360
by bringing in Ludwig Wittgenstein.

03:23.360 --> 03:26.320
Oh, man, Wittgenstein.

03:26.320 --> 03:30.080
Arguably the most intense philosopher of language from the 20th century.

03:30.080 --> 03:32.640
He wrote this notoriously dense book that tracked at us,

03:32.640 --> 03:35.040
which is basically impossible to read casually.

03:35.040 --> 03:35.760
Oh, completely.

03:35.760 --> 03:37.360
And he concluded it with this famous line,

03:37.920 --> 03:39.680
where of one cannot speak,

03:39.680 --> 03:41.520
there of one must be silent.

03:41.520 --> 03:42.960
So a lot of thinkers read that line,

03:43.040 --> 03:47.120
and they assume Wittgenstein reached the summit of human thought.

03:47.120 --> 03:49.440
They think he arrived at the same mystical,

03:49.440 --> 03:52.400
transcendent silence that Eastern contemplative traditions

03:52.400 --> 03:53.760
have been teaching for millennia.

03:53.760 --> 03:56.240
But the author makes a brilliant distinction here.

03:56.240 --> 03:59.120
Wittgenstein didn't actually cross into the silence.

03:59.120 --> 04:01.120
He just drew a map of the noise.

04:01.120 --> 04:03.440
He mapped the boundary from the inside.

04:03.440 --> 04:04.640
Exactly.

04:04.640 --> 04:09.520
He used logic to push language to its absolute breaking point,

04:10.080 --> 04:13.280
drew a circle around everything we can rationally talk about,

04:13.280 --> 04:14.800
and basically said,

04:14.800 --> 04:17.200
OK, outside this circle is the unspeakable.

04:17.200 --> 04:19.120
Don't even try to go there.

04:19.120 --> 04:22.160
He's like a cartographer drawing dragons at the edge of the map,

04:22.160 --> 04:25.440
but he never actually sails the ship into those uncharted waters.

04:25.440 --> 04:28.960
He stays totally safe inside the realm of logic.

04:28.960 --> 04:32.560
He's essentially saying that language is a logical picture of facts.

04:33.200 --> 04:36.720
And since the meaning of the world lies outside the facts of the world,

04:36.720 --> 04:38.080
language just can't touch it.

04:38.080 --> 04:39.680
It's a brilliant diagnosis.

04:39.680 --> 04:42.000
It is, but it's still just a diagnosis.

04:42.000 --> 04:43.840
The whole climbing of a ladder,

04:43.840 --> 04:46.880
then throwing away the ladder that Wittgenstein talks about,

04:46.880 --> 04:49.520
it remains a purely intellectual exercise.

04:49.520 --> 04:52.160
Right, to actually experience what lies beyond that boundary,

04:52.160 --> 04:54.800
we have to look at the philosophical escalation that happens

04:54.800 --> 04:57.360
when thinkers try to break out of the map entirely.

04:57.360 --> 04:58.800
Unless trace that escalation,

04:58.800 --> 05:00.960
because the author lays it up beautifully,

05:00.960 --> 05:03.600
we start with Wittgenstein pointing at the boundary.

05:03.600 --> 05:05.760
The next massive leap is Edmund Hussle.

05:05.760 --> 05:07.200
The father of phenomenology.

05:07.200 --> 05:09.200
Right. Hussle isn't just drawing a map.

05:09.200 --> 05:13.040
He's basically inventing a new way to use your brain.

05:13.040 --> 05:15.360
He develops this concept called the epique,

05:15.360 --> 05:19.120
which is usually translated as bracketing the natural attitude, right?

05:19.120 --> 05:22.240
Yeah, bracketing, which I know sounds like an accounting term,

05:22.240 --> 05:24.080
but let's actually walk through what that feels like.

05:24.080 --> 05:26.400
Yeah, please, because it's a weird concept.

05:26.400 --> 05:28.080
So normally you move through the world,

05:28.880 --> 05:30.480
taking reality totally of a granted.

05:31.280 --> 05:33.840
You look at, say, a coffee cup on your desk,

05:34.720 --> 05:36.800
and your brain instantly labels it.

05:37.360 --> 05:38.880
My favorite ceramic cup.

05:38.880 --> 05:40.320
It holds hot liquid.

05:40.320 --> 05:42.400
It exists subjectively in space.

05:42.400 --> 05:46.240
You are basically drowning in assumptions and histories and categories.

05:46.240 --> 05:47.280
You're not seeing the cup.

05:47.280 --> 05:49.040
You're seeing the concept of a cup.

05:49.040 --> 05:49.920
Exactly.

05:49.920 --> 05:53.680
Hussle's epique is the practice of mentally putting all those concepts.

05:53.680 --> 05:55.200
The ceramic part, the utility,

05:55.200 --> 05:59.120
even the belief that the cup exists independently of you into brackets.

05:59.120 --> 06:00.880
You suspend your judgment entirely.

06:00.880 --> 06:02.960
Like pausing the mental software.

06:02.960 --> 06:03.920
Yes.

06:03.920 --> 06:06.800
You stop analyzing what the object is in the real world.

06:07.360 --> 06:10.240
And you force your attention onto the pure,

06:10.240 --> 06:14.080
raw phenomenon of the object appearing in your consciousness.

06:14.080 --> 06:16.080
It's a massive mental shift.

06:16.080 --> 06:19.360
You're no longer playing Vickinstein's logic game at all.

06:19.360 --> 06:24.000
You're doing something closer to like an intense,

06:24.000 --> 06:25.760
hyper-focused meditation.

06:25.760 --> 06:28.080
You are training a specific mode of attention

06:28.080 --> 06:29.600
to bypass the labels.

06:29.600 --> 06:32.000
And that leads directly to the next escalation,

06:32.000 --> 06:33.120
which is Martin Heidegger,

06:33.120 --> 06:35.040
who is actually Hussle's student.

06:35.040 --> 06:36.560
Heidegger takes this practice

06:36.560 --> 06:39.600
and applies it to the very nature of existence itself.

06:39.600 --> 06:41.760
He introduces concepts like Alaskan height,

06:41.760 --> 06:44.880
which translates roughly to letting B or releasement.

06:44.880 --> 06:48.720
Heidegger wanted to dynamite the entire history of Western philosophy, didn't he?

06:48.720 --> 06:51.280
Because he thought it was obsessed with categorizing things

06:51.280 --> 06:53.440
as objects to be studied by subjects.

06:53.440 --> 06:54.480
He really did.

06:54.480 --> 06:57.440
He wanted to get before the split between the subject and the object.

06:57.440 --> 06:59.440
Okay, so think about the moments in your life

06:59.440 --> 07:01.920
when you are completely immersed in an activity.

07:01.920 --> 07:06.000
Maybe you're playing a piano or surfing or coding in a deep flow state.

07:06.080 --> 07:07.760
Flow state is the perfect example.

07:07.760 --> 07:11.280
In those moments, there is no you analyzing the piano.

07:11.280 --> 07:13.120
The boundary just vanishes.

07:13.120 --> 07:14.960
There is just the music happening.

07:14.960 --> 07:16.480
Heidegger believed that this state

07:16.480 --> 07:18.000
getting behind that artificial wall

07:18.000 --> 07:20.080
between observer and observed

07:20.080 --> 07:24.080
was the only way to glimpse being with a capital B.

07:24.080 --> 07:26.160
It's really no surprise that in his later years,

07:26.160 --> 07:29.040
his work started to deeply echo East Asian thought,

07:29.040 --> 07:31.200
especially Taoist philosophy.

07:31.200 --> 07:34.880
Which brings us to the absolute peak of this progression in the essay.

07:34.880 --> 07:37.840
We go from Vickinstein mapping the limits of words

07:37.840 --> 07:41.920
to hostile suspending labels to Heidegger dissolving the observer

07:41.920 --> 07:44.000
and finally to the Buddha.

07:44.000 --> 07:47.760
And the author argues that the Buddha wasn't just theorizing about any of this.

07:47.760 --> 07:50.480
The Buddha was pure unadulterated practice.

07:50.480 --> 07:52.960
There is this stunning historical detail

07:52.960 --> 07:54.960
the author includes to illustrate this.

07:54.960 --> 07:57.440
After wandering and teaching for 45 years,

07:57.440 --> 08:00.160
delivering thousands of discourses to his followers,

08:00.160 --> 08:02.960
the Buddha is said to have declared on his deathbed.

08:03.040 --> 08:04.960
I have not said a single word.

08:04.960 --> 08:05.920
Which is wild.

08:05.920 --> 08:09.360
Right. If you look at that through Vickinstein's purely logical lens,

08:09.360 --> 08:11.440
it's nonsense. It's a blatant contradiction.

08:11.440 --> 08:13.600
The guy talked for four and a half decades.

08:13.600 --> 08:16.080
But if you understand the escalation we just mapped out,

08:16.720 --> 08:19.280
it is the most coherent thing he could possibly say.

08:20.160 --> 08:23.840
Those 45 years of speaking were never meant to be objective truths.

08:24.720 --> 08:27.360
They were, in the classic ven metaphor,

08:27.360 --> 08:29.280
merely fingers pointing at the moon.

08:29.760 --> 08:31.680
The words are the finger.

08:31.680 --> 08:34.240
The direct experience of reality is the moon.

08:34.240 --> 08:37.600
If you stare at the finger, you miss the sky completely.

08:37.600 --> 08:40.400
The author brings in the diamond sutra to drive this home.

08:40.400 --> 08:43.440
It's perhaps the most famous text in Mahayana Buddhism.

08:43.440 --> 08:47.680
And the entire sutra is a relentless assault on language using language.

08:47.680 --> 08:50.000
Exactly. It repeats over and over

08:50.000 --> 08:53.520
that all forms or illusions that the Buddha has no fixed teaching,

08:53.520 --> 08:57.280
that saying you have achieved enlightenment actually proves you haven't.

08:57.280 --> 09:01.120
It operates entirely on the principle of direct seeing the Chinese character Guan.

09:01.120 --> 09:05.040
Guan. It's perception without the filter of vocabulary.

09:05.520 --> 09:08.880
So to bring this back to you, the listener, we have a problem here today.

09:08.880 --> 09:10.880
We have this time together to do a deep dive.

09:10.880 --> 09:15.360
And the only tool we have to connect with you is the very language we just proved is flawed.

09:15.360 --> 09:16.880
We are in the language trap.

09:17.520 --> 09:20.160
And the author acknowledges this trap openly in the text.

09:20.160 --> 09:22.720
They write, I will use language in what follows.

09:22.720 --> 09:23.920
I have no choice.

09:23.920 --> 09:25.920
We are in the exact same boat.

09:26.000 --> 09:30.000
We have to use the scaffolding of words to build these concepts for you.

09:30.000 --> 09:34.000
But the critical takeaway for you listening is how you process what you're hearing.

09:34.480 --> 09:39.680
The words we're using today are just the exhaust fumes of the actual realization.

09:39.680 --> 09:41.840
Oh, I like that. The exhaust fumes.

09:41.840 --> 09:45.360
Right. They are the footprints in the dirt, not the creature that left them.

09:45.360 --> 09:49.120
So as we move into the actual dismantling of human meaning,

09:49.120 --> 09:55.360
try to look past our syntax, look at the vast, terrifying, empty space the words are pointing toward.

09:55.360 --> 09:59.760
With that caveat in place, we can pick up the actual instrument the author uses

09:59.760 --> 10:01.920
to dissect human experience.

10:01.920 --> 10:05.200
Because if words are flawed, what internal mechanism do we rely on?

10:05.200 --> 10:07.360
The author chooses rational thinking.

10:07.360 --> 10:11.440
But they immediately strict away all the romantic mythology we usually attach to it.

10:11.440 --> 10:14.720
This was a major wake-up call for me reading the text.

10:14.720 --> 10:19.200
We have this deep-seated, incredibly flattering self-image in Western culture.

10:19.840 --> 10:24.880
We think of ourselves as these autonomous, free thinking captains of our own destiny.

10:24.880 --> 10:28.640
We really believe our rationality makes us independent from the universe.

10:28.640 --> 10:31.600
The author takes a sledgehammer to that illusion.

10:31.600 --> 10:34.080
Strict autonomy simply does not exist.

10:34.080 --> 10:34.720
It's a fiction.

10:35.600 --> 10:40.800
You are an organism shaped by a relentless barrage of external forces,

10:40.800 --> 10:46.240
your environment, your genetic inheritance, the specific grammatical structure of your native language.

10:46.240 --> 10:48.960
The unspoken belief systems of your zip code.

10:48.960 --> 10:53.040
Yes. All of these things have been stamped on your nervous system without your consent.

10:53.040 --> 10:56.080
It really makes you question every original thought you've ever had.

10:56.080 --> 10:59.600
Like, when I think I'm making a deeply personal independent choice,

10:59.600 --> 11:04.880
I'm usually just executing a complex software program written by my culture and my biology.

11:04.880 --> 11:07.200
Your originality is largely a mirage.

11:07.200 --> 11:09.680
But wait, this is where I push back when I was reading.

11:09.680 --> 11:15.200
If my free will is an illusion, and I'm basically just a meat computer running cultural software,

11:15.840 --> 11:18.160
why bother trying to think rationally at all?

11:18.160 --> 11:20.560
Aren't I just running on a pre-programmed track?

11:20.560 --> 11:21.600
It's a fair question.

11:21.600 --> 11:24.640
Why not just let your environment pull the levers and go to sleep at the wheel?

11:24.640 --> 11:25.520
Exactly.

11:25.520 --> 11:29.840
But the author's defense of rationality here is brilliantly unromantic.

11:29.840 --> 11:31.200
It's pure pragmatism.

11:31.920 --> 11:36.240
We don't use rationality because it grants us some magical metaphysical free will.

11:36.240 --> 11:42.240
We use it because it's empirically the best tool for achieving very specific outcomes.

11:42.240 --> 11:44.640
They lay out two concrete criteria.

11:44.640 --> 11:49.280
We use reason because it maximizes our ability to make our own lives unfold

11:49.280 --> 11:50.960
in ways we find desirable.

11:50.960 --> 11:56.080
And secondly, it maximizes our ability to ensure our actions do good for humanity

11:56.080 --> 11:57.600
and the broader universe.

11:57.600 --> 11:59.040
Notice what's missing there, though.

11:59.040 --> 12:01.680
The author isn't claiming that making your life desirable

12:01.680 --> 12:04.160
is objectively true with a capital T.

12:04.160 --> 12:05.120
No, not at all.

12:05.120 --> 12:07.040
It's just saying, look, since you're here,

12:07.040 --> 12:10.480
and since you clearly prefer feeling good over feeling terrible,

12:10.480 --> 12:13.360
and preferring a functioning society over a collapsed one,

12:13.840 --> 12:17.200
rationality is the sharpest tool in the shed to get you there.

12:17.200 --> 12:19.760
The alternative is relying on emotional reasoning.

12:20.400 --> 12:22.960
And the author issues a severe warning about that.

12:23.840 --> 12:28.640
Relying on your emotions to decide what is honorable or meaningful is a total trap.

12:28.640 --> 12:32.720
Because your emotions are the very things that have been programmed by your environment.

12:32.720 --> 12:33.360
Exactly.

12:33.360 --> 12:36.320
If you rely on a gut feeling to tell you what's noble,

12:36.320 --> 12:39.680
your gut is just going to regurgitate whatever society fed it.

12:39.680 --> 12:43.360
Right. Like if you're born into a hyper-militaristic society,

12:43.360 --> 12:46.880
your emotions will naturally swell with pride at the thought of dying

12:46.880 --> 12:48.560
in a completely pointless war.

12:48.560 --> 12:50.800
Or if you're born into modern hustle culture,

12:50.800 --> 12:53.440
your emotions will convince you that sacrificing your physical health

12:53.440 --> 12:56.080
for a corporate promotion is the highest form of honor.

12:56.560 --> 12:59.520
The author summarizes this with a chilling phrase.

12:59.520 --> 13:05.280
Relying purely on inherited emotional responses is being thought by one's environment.

13:05.280 --> 13:06.960
Being thought by one's environment.

13:07.600 --> 13:09.280
You aren't generating thoughts.

13:09.280 --> 13:12.240
The environment is using your brain to generate its thoughts.

13:12.240 --> 13:15.040
Your mind is just a colony for cultural viruses.

13:15.040 --> 13:16.880
So rationality is our scalpel.

13:17.440 --> 13:21.120
It's the only instrument sharp enough to cut through the dense layers

13:21.120 --> 13:23.840
of inherited programming and sever the puppet strings.

13:23.840 --> 13:25.760
And that brings us to the core of the essay.

13:25.760 --> 13:26.640
We have our scalpel.

13:27.280 --> 13:31.360
The author asks us to take this tool of pragmatic rationality

13:31.360 --> 13:34.400
and apply it ruthlessly to everything we consider sacred,

13:34.400 --> 13:37.120
beautiful, and honorable in the human experience.

13:37.120 --> 13:38.640
We are stepping into the dissolution.

13:38.640 --> 13:39.920
We are falling into the void.

13:40.480 --> 13:44.560
And the author goes straight for the throat by dissecting romantic love.

13:44.640 --> 13:45.680
Yeah, they don't hold back.

13:45.680 --> 13:47.920
Think about the pedestal we put romantic love on.

13:48.560 --> 13:51.920
Thousands of years of poetry, Shakespeare, pop music.

13:51.920 --> 13:53.520
Romeo and Juliet, dying for each other,

13:53.520 --> 13:56.640
is basically the ultimate cultural symbol of nobility.

13:56.640 --> 14:00.560
Love is supposedly this cosmic force that transcends time and space.

14:01.120 --> 14:02.560
But let's apply this scalpel.

14:02.560 --> 14:02.880
Yeah.

14:02.880 --> 14:04.720
What happens when you look at romantic love

14:04.720 --> 14:07.040
through the cold lens of rational analysis?

14:07.040 --> 14:08.960
The poetry just evaporates.

14:08.960 --> 14:11.120
And you were left with evolutionary biology.

14:11.120 --> 14:14.320
It's just neurochemistry and DNA trying to propagate itself.

14:14.720 --> 14:19.040
The intense gravitational pull you feel toward another human being

14:19.040 --> 14:21.440
is a highly optimized survival mechanism.

14:22.400 --> 14:25.440
We feel romantic love because the specific DNA sequences

14:25.440 --> 14:27.920
that predisposed our early ancestors to bond,

14:27.920 --> 14:30.160
stay together and protect their offspring

14:30.160 --> 14:34.000
were the sequences that survived the brutal filter of natural selection.

14:34.000 --> 14:35.760
The ancestors who didn't fall in love

14:35.760 --> 14:38.560
who felt no biological urge to protect their mates,

14:38.560 --> 14:40.880
they simply didn't pass on their genes.

14:40.880 --> 14:44.160
Their indifference was bred out of the gene pool entirely.

14:44.160 --> 14:46.400
So that mystical feeling of two souls

14:46.400 --> 14:48.080
finding each other in the dark,

14:48.080 --> 14:51.200
it's a blind, unthinking optimization process.

14:51.840 --> 14:53.680
Juliet is just a biological vessel

14:53.680 --> 14:56.800
for Romeo's genetic material to replicate and vice versa.

14:56.800 --> 14:59.920
Which is such a brutally unromantic thing to say out loud.

14:59.920 --> 15:01.200
Imagine telling your partner,

15:01.200 --> 15:04.720
hey, my DNA just really wants to replicate with yours.

15:04.720 --> 15:05.760
I wouldn't recommend it.

15:05.760 --> 15:07.840
But the scalpel doesn't stop at romance.

15:07.840 --> 15:10.720
It cuts through the entire spectrum of human emotion.

15:10.800 --> 15:14.400
Joy, sorrow, righteous anger, empathy,

15:14.400 --> 15:16.480
even the feeling of profound inspiration.

15:16.480 --> 15:17.760
That one hurts the most, I think.

15:18.320 --> 15:22.160
The idea that feeling inspired to create art or build something new

15:22.160 --> 15:23.840
isn't a touch of the divine,

15:23.840 --> 15:25.520
but just another survival tool.

15:26.000 --> 15:28.960
Feelings of ambition or inspiration drive organisms

15:28.960 --> 15:31.200
to overcome environmental obstacles,

15:31.200 --> 15:34.080
secure more resources and elevate their status

15:34.080 --> 15:35.600
within a social hierarchy.

15:35.600 --> 15:39.680
And all of that drastically improves the odds of survival and reproduction.

15:40.480 --> 15:43.600
Every emotion, no matter how sacred it feels from the inside,

15:44.240 --> 15:46.880
is just a nervous system configuration designed

15:46.880 --> 15:48.640
to navigate a physical environment.

15:48.640 --> 15:51.600
The universe doesn't care if you feel joy or despair.

15:51.600 --> 15:54.240
Your brain just uses those emotional states

15:54.240 --> 15:56.000
as internal carrots and sticks.

15:56.000 --> 15:59.840
There is absolutely no inherent cosmic honor in any of it.

15:59.840 --> 16:02.240
Let's turn the scalpel toward another massive pillar

16:02.240 --> 16:05.360
of human meaning, greatness and ambition.

16:05.360 --> 16:07.520
We build monuments to historical figures.

16:07.520 --> 16:09.040
We obsess over visionaries.

16:09.120 --> 16:11.680
The author points to figures like tech CEO Jensen Huang

16:11.680 --> 16:12.640
or Albert Einstein.

16:13.200 --> 16:16.320
We treat their achievements driving technological innovation,

16:16.320 --> 16:17.760
unraveling the laws of physics

16:17.760 --> 16:20.000
as the absolute pinnacle of human worth.

16:20.000 --> 16:21.040
But why?

16:21.040 --> 16:24.720
Why is a human building a supercomputer inherently more valuable

16:24.720 --> 16:26.640
or honorable than a beaver building a dam?

16:27.360 --> 16:30.240
Or, frankly, more honorable than the intricate

16:30.240 --> 16:33.200
mathematical perfection of a hurricane forming over the ocean?

16:33.200 --> 16:35.280
When you trace the logic all the way down,

16:36.000 --> 16:38.960
you find that our worship of human greatness

16:38.960 --> 16:40.800
is a completely closed loop.

16:40.800 --> 16:44.240
We honor human achievements because we are humans,

16:44.240 --> 16:46.720
operating inside a human-centric culture

16:46.720 --> 16:49.600
that invented the concept of achievement in the first place.

16:49.600 --> 16:51.760
We built the game, wrote the rules,

16:51.760 --> 16:53.040
designed the scoreboard,

16:53.040 --> 16:56.000
and now we bow down to the people who rack up the most points.

16:56.000 --> 16:56.960
It's totally circular.

16:56.960 --> 16:59.360
We honor this because this is what our species does.

16:59.360 --> 17:01.920
There's no foundation beneath the four boards,

17:01.920 --> 17:04.240
which naturally leads people to try and zoom out, right?

17:04.320 --> 17:05.840
When individual meaning fails,

17:05.840 --> 17:07.440
we usually try to attach ourselves

17:07.440 --> 17:09.680
to a massive collective philosophy.

17:09.680 --> 17:10.800
The isms.

17:10.800 --> 17:13.440
Humanism is the dominant one in the secular world.

17:13.440 --> 17:15.040
The belief that the ultimate good

17:15.040 --> 17:17.280
is the flourishing and progress of the human race.

17:17.280 --> 17:18.560
It sounds unassailable.

17:18.560 --> 17:21.920
I mean, who wants to argue against the flourishing of humanity?

17:21.920 --> 17:24.240
But the scalpel demands we ask.

17:24.240 --> 17:25.520
Why humanism?

17:25.520 --> 17:28.000
If you are relying purely on rationality,

17:28.000 --> 17:30.160
you cannot logically prove that humanism

17:30.160 --> 17:31.680
is the correct viewpoint.

17:31.680 --> 17:33.120
Why not animalism,

17:33.120 --> 17:37.120
where we value the flourishing of all sentient life equally,

17:37.120 --> 17:38.640
which would mean human convenience

17:38.640 --> 17:41.520
must be radically sacrificed for the comfort of livestock,

17:41.520 --> 17:43.760
or take it even further to what the author calls,

17:43.760 --> 17:45.040
Earthism,

17:45.040 --> 17:48.080
valuing the flourishing of the entire planetary ecosystem

17:48.080 --> 17:49.760
above any single species.

17:49.760 --> 17:51.520
If you adopt Earthism,

17:51.520 --> 17:54.000
humanism suddenly looks like a massive threat.

17:54.000 --> 17:57.040
The author brings in a phenomenal pop culture reference here,

17:57.040 --> 17:58.560
Agent Smith from The Matrix.

17:58.560 --> 17:59.840
No, I love this part.

17:59.840 --> 18:02.160
Agent Smith famously interrogates Morpheus

18:02.160 --> 18:04.400
and describes humanity not as a mammal,

18:04.400 --> 18:05.760
but as a virus,

18:05.760 --> 18:06.800
a cancer of the planet

18:06.800 --> 18:08.560
that endlessly consumes resources

18:08.560 --> 18:09.920
until the host is dead.

18:09.920 --> 18:11.760
From the perspective of Earthism,

18:11.760 --> 18:12.800
wiping out humanity

18:12.800 --> 18:15.520
might be the most honorable, rational course of action.

18:15.520 --> 18:17.040
Or what about alienism?

18:17.040 --> 18:18.720
The idea that we should prioritize

18:18.720 --> 18:20.480
the flourishing of some hyper-advanced

18:20.480 --> 18:21.840
intergalactic intelligence

18:21.840 --> 18:23.680
that views us the way we view ants.

18:23.680 --> 18:25.280
The underlying philosophical point

18:25.280 --> 18:27.040
is that none of these elizisms

18:27.040 --> 18:28.800
can be grounded in anything deeper

18:28.800 --> 18:30.560
than an arbitrary starting premise.

18:31.360 --> 18:32.960
Rationality is a mechanism

18:32.960 --> 18:35.520
for moving from premise A to conclusion B.

18:35.520 --> 18:37.200
It cannot prove premise A.

18:37.200 --> 18:38.560
You have to blindly assume

18:38.560 --> 18:40.560
that human life is the most valuable thing

18:40.560 --> 18:42.480
in order to argue for humanism.

18:42.480 --> 18:43.760
You can't use logic

18:43.760 --> 18:45.120
to prove the very premise

18:45.120 --> 18:46.720
that logic requires to function.

18:46.720 --> 18:48.640
And this is the moment we hit the bottom.

18:48.640 --> 18:50.640
We have deconstructed love,

18:50.640 --> 18:51.840
emotion,

18:51.840 --> 18:52.960
greatness,

18:52.960 --> 18:55.040
and every grand philosophy.

18:55.040 --> 18:56.800
Every single standpoint dissolves

18:56.800 --> 18:58.160
under the pressure of the scalpel.

18:58.160 --> 18:59.600
The author states the reality

18:59.600 --> 19:02.160
of the situation with brutal clarity.

19:02.160 --> 19:03.520
There is, in this sense,

19:03.520 --> 19:05.920
nothing honorable to do in this world period.

19:05.920 --> 19:06.720
I want to pause here

19:06.720 --> 19:08.880
because it is so easy to misinterpret this

19:08.880 --> 19:10.960
as just dark edgy nihilism.

19:10.960 --> 19:11.280
Right.

19:11.280 --> 19:13.440
It feels like we're just saying everything is trash.

19:13.440 --> 19:15.280
But the author leans on David Hume,

19:15.280 --> 19:17.280
the 18th century Scottish philosopher,

19:17.840 --> 19:19.840
and his famous isot distinction

19:19.840 --> 19:21.440
to clarify this void.

19:21.440 --> 19:23.520
Hume's insight is crucial here.

19:23.520 --> 19:25.760
He pointed out that you cannot deduce

19:25.760 --> 19:26.800
an ought,

19:26.800 --> 19:28.080
a moral imperative

19:28.240 --> 19:29.680
about how things should be

19:29.680 --> 19:30.880
from an is,

19:30.880 --> 19:33.360
a factual observation about how things are.

19:33.360 --> 19:35.040
Just because we use the scalpel

19:35.040 --> 19:37.120
to discover that love is a biological trick

19:37.120 --> 19:38.640
to ensure reproduction

19:38.640 --> 19:40.320
doesn't automatically mean love

19:40.320 --> 19:42.160
ought to be abandoned as worthless.

19:42.160 --> 19:44.480
The fact that our values have a contingent,

19:44.480 --> 19:46.640
evolutionary, random origin

19:46.640 --> 19:49.120
doesn't automatically dictate their normative status.

19:49.120 --> 19:51.280
It doesn't mean they are inherently trash.

19:51.280 --> 19:53.840
The specific phrasing the author uses is vital.

19:54.560 --> 19:56.000
These things aren't meaningless.

19:56.000 --> 19:57.840
They just lack a cosmic warrant.

19:57.840 --> 19:59.200
A cosmic warrant.

19:59.200 --> 20:00.480
I love that term.

20:00.480 --> 20:02.080
The universe didn't sign a certificate

20:02.080 --> 20:03.520
guaranteeing that your ambition

20:03.520 --> 20:05.920
or your compassion has objective value.

20:05.920 --> 20:08.480
There is no supreme judge keeping score.

20:08.480 --> 20:09.520
The void is real,

20:09.520 --> 20:10.720
the universe is silent,

20:10.720 --> 20:12.000
and there is no safety net.

20:12.000 --> 20:13.120
And if we stop here,

20:13.120 --> 20:14.320
we are stranded.

20:14.320 --> 20:15.680
We're completely paralyzed

20:15.680 --> 20:17.360
and pure nihilism.

20:17.360 --> 20:18.880
But the author makes a fascinating

20:18.880 --> 20:20.640
self-correction regarding their own

20:20.640 --> 20:21.920
intellectual journey.

20:21.920 --> 20:23.280
They realize that reason got them

20:23.280 --> 20:24.320
to the bottom of the void,

20:24.320 --> 20:26.320
but it fundamentally lacked the machinery

20:26.320 --> 20:27.200
to get them out.

20:27.280 --> 20:29.040
If rationality is so powerful,

20:29.040 --> 20:30.400
why can't it rebuild meaning?

20:30.960 --> 20:33.360
Because of the nature of the tool itself,

20:33.360 --> 20:35.200
rationality is a scalpel.

20:35.200 --> 20:36.720
It is an instrument of division,

20:36.720 --> 20:37.920
of dissection.

20:37.920 --> 20:39.520
It cuts away disease tissue

20:39.520 --> 20:41.200
and severs illusions,

20:41.200 --> 20:43.920
but you cannot use a scalpel to grow a garden.

20:43.920 --> 20:46.320
You cannot deduce your way into a meaningful life.

20:46.320 --> 20:48.080
If you rely purely on logic,

20:48.080 --> 20:50.000
you just stand in an empty field,

20:50.000 --> 20:51.360
holding a sharp knife,

20:51.360 --> 20:53.280
surrounded by the shredded pieces

20:53.280 --> 20:54.640
of your cultural programming.

20:54.640 --> 20:57.280
Rationality cleared the plot of land perfectly,

20:57.280 --> 20:59.040
but it has no seeds to plant.

20:59.040 --> 21:00.320
The return to living

21:00.320 --> 21:02.560
requires a completely different mechanism.

21:02.560 --> 21:03.920
We need a second engine.

21:03.920 --> 21:05.520
To define the second engine,

21:05.520 --> 21:08.240
the author points to what we broadly call wisdom

21:08.240 --> 21:10.000
and draws on three distinct

21:10.000 --> 21:12.080
philosophical traditions to explain it.

21:12.080 --> 21:14.800
First is Aristotle's concept of frenesis

21:14.800 --> 21:16.160
or practical wisdom.

21:16.160 --> 21:18.720
This isn't book smarts or logical deduction.

21:18.720 --> 21:20.720
It's the lived intuitive knowledge

21:20.720 --> 21:22.400
of how to act in complex,

21:22.400 --> 21:24.240
mythy human situations

21:24.240 --> 21:26.000
where strict rules break down.

21:26.000 --> 21:28.480
Second, they invoke Jean-Paul Sart

21:28.480 --> 21:30.560
and the existentialist tradition.

21:30.560 --> 21:32.240
The core of existentialism

21:32.240 --> 21:35.120
is that existence precedes essence.

21:35.120 --> 21:37.120
You exist first in the void

21:37.120 --> 21:39.280
and you must actively choose your essence.

21:39.280 --> 21:42.000
You don't discover your path through logic.

21:42.000 --> 21:44.800
You forge it through bold, unjustified choice.

21:44.800 --> 21:47.360
And the third, which I think is just the most beautiful,

21:47.360 --> 21:49.680
comes back to Mahayana Buddhism.

21:49.680 --> 21:51.200
It's the idea of compassion,

21:51.360 --> 21:53.120
that arises spontaneously

21:53.120 --> 21:55.200
from the realization of emptiness calm.

21:55.200 --> 21:57.440
In the West, we usually equate emptiness

21:57.440 --> 21:58.800
with a cold dead vacuum.

21:59.760 --> 22:01.040
But in this tradition,

22:01.040 --> 22:02.400
truly realizing the void,

22:02.400 --> 22:04.720
realizing that you and everyone around you

22:04.720 --> 22:08.400
lack any permanent, inherent self or cosmic armor.

22:08.400 --> 22:09.760
Doesn't breed apathy.

22:09.760 --> 22:11.840
It breeds profound empathy.

22:11.840 --> 22:14.240
You see the sheer vulnerability of being alive

22:14.240 --> 22:16.160
and compassion is the natural response

22:16.160 --> 22:18.720
of an awakened mind to that shared vulnerability.

22:18.720 --> 22:20.000
The author gives us an analogy

22:20.000 --> 22:21.840
that perfectly unifies all these concepts.

22:21.840 --> 22:22.320
Yeah.

22:22.320 --> 22:23.520
Reason is the scalpel.

22:23.520 --> 22:25.840
Wisdom is the hand that knows when to put the scalpel down.

22:25.840 --> 22:27.840
It perfectly captures the tension.

22:28.400 --> 22:30.720
I want you to think about this dynamic in your own life.

22:31.440 --> 22:32.960
We all know someone or maybe,

22:32.960 --> 22:33.840
you know, we've been this person

22:33.840 --> 22:35.360
who cannot put the scalpel down.

22:35.360 --> 22:37.200
They hyperanalyze everything.

22:37.200 --> 22:37.920
Everything.

22:37.920 --> 22:40.320
They dissect their romantic relationships,

22:40.320 --> 22:42.240
reading into every single text message

22:42.240 --> 22:43.600
and tone of voice,

22:43.600 --> 22:46.640
logically deconstructing their partners' motives

22:46.640 --> 22:48.720
until the relationship essentially

22:48.720 --> 22:50.560
bleeds out on the operating table.

22:50.560 --> 22:52.560
Or they analyze a career passion

22:52.560 --> 22:54.000
until they logically prove it's

22:54.000 --> 22:56.080
statistically unlikely to succeed

22:56.080 --> 22:57.360
and they lose all joy in it.

22:57.360 --> 23:00.000
The purely rational person stays in the void

23:00.000 --> 23:01.760
shredding everything they touch.

23:01.760 --> 23:04.160
But the wise person uses the scalpel

23:04.160 --> 23:06.080
to cut away toxic illusions

23:06.080 --> 23:07.600
like the pressure to conform

23:07.600 --> 23:09.920
to society's definition of success

23:09.920 --> 23:12.240
and then deliberately puts the blade away.

23:12.240 --> 23:14.960
They consciously decide to experience the relationship.

23:15.040 --> 23:16.800
They choose to paint the canvas,

23:16.800 --> 23:18.640
knowing the cosmos doesn't care,

23:18.640 --> 23:19.920
but choosing to care anyway.

23:19.920 --> 23:22.160
Which brings us to the actual act of living,

23:22.160 --> 23:23.840
what the author calls the return.

23:24.400 --> 23:26.160
With the scalpel safely sheebed,

23:26.160 --> 23:28.320
how do we step back out into the world?

23:28.320 --> 23:29.520
Having looked into the void

23:29.520 --> 23:31.840
and accepted that no value survives

23:31.840 --> 23:33.600
strict rational scrutiny,

23:33.600 --> 23:36.160
the author's response is incredibly grounding.

23:36.960 --> 23:38.160
I came back to the human.

23:38.800 --> 23:40.400
Back to the messy, illogical,

23:40.400 --> 23:43.280
contingent attachments of an actual life,

23:43.280 --> 23:44.560
back to loving your family,

23:44.640 --> 23:45.920
caring about your friends,

23:45.920 --> 23:47.200
crying at movies,

23:47.200 --> 23:50.480
but the entire context of why we do these things

23:50.480 --> 23:52.080
has fundamentally shifted.

23:52.080 --> 23:53.520
We aren't returning to these things

23:53.520 --> 23:56.640
because we suddenly found a hidden cosmic warrant for them.

23:56.640 --> 23:59.040
We already prove that doesn't exist.

23:59.040 --> 24:01.440
We return to them simply because we are human.

24:01.440 --> 24:03.440
There is no view from nowhere.

24:03.440 --> 24:04.880
We can't float in the void

24:04.880 --> 24:07.040
as pure nodes of disembodied logic.

24:07.040 --> 24:08.400
We are flesh, blood,

24:08.400 --> 24:10.560
neurochemistry, and social needs.

24:10.560 --> 24:14.160
We are thrown in this specific biological and cultural form.

24:14.160 --> 24:17.600
The human experience is the only material we have to work with.

24:17.600 --> 24:20.000
So these connections and emotions become valuable,

24:20.000 --> 24:22.400
not because they are objective universal truths,

24:22.400 --> 24:24.560
but because they are the only ground available

24:24.560 --> 24:26.160
for a human to stand on.

24:26.160 --> 24:28.480
To really nail down the psychology of this return,

24:28.480 --> 24:30.480
the author tinds to Seren Kierkegaard,

24:30.480 --> 24:32.240
the 19th century Danish philosopher

24:32.240 --> 24:34.480
and his concept of the Knight of Faith.

24:34.480 --> 24:38.000
Kierkegaard describes a profound double movement.

24:38.720 --> 24:42.240
The first movement is infinite resignation,

24:42.320 --> 24:44.400
which is everything we did with the scalpel,

24:44.400 --> 24:46.640
surrendering our demand for cosmic meaning,

24:46.640 --> 24:48.160
accepting the void,

24:48.160 --> 24:51.840
resigning ourselves to the fact that the universe owes us absolutely nothing.

24:51.840 --> 24:53.520
A lot of philosophers stop there.

24:53.520 --> 24:56.000
They become knights of infinite resignation,

24:56.000 --> 24:57.920
detached, cynical,

24:57.920 --> 24:59.440
hovering above the world.

24:59.440 --> 25:02.480
But the Knight of Faith makes the crucial second movement,

25:03.520 --> 25:06.880
a total passionate return to the finite world.

25:06.880 --> 25:10.240
The Knight of Faith dives back into the messy reality of life

25:10.240 --> 25:13.120
and loves it completely as if it had absolute meaning

25:13.120 --> 25:16.320
while simultaneously knowing with perfect clarity that it does not.

25:16.320 --> 25:18.800
It's a paradox. Total release,

25:18.800 --> 25:20.720
followed immediately by total engagement.

25:21.360 --> 25:24.240
Now, when I first read this, my immediate reaction was skepticism.

25:24.240 --> 25:26.720
Doesn't this just sound like an intellectual cop out?

25:26.720 --> 25:28.880
Aren't we just plugging ourselves back into the matrix

25:28.880 --> 25:32.320
because the cold, hard desert of the real world was too depressing?

25:32.320 --> 25:34.720
It feels like we're just choosing to be naive again.

25:34.720 --> 25:36.960
It's the most common critique of existentialism.

25:36.960 --> 25:38.960
Yeah, how is pretending something matters

25:38.960 --> 25:40.800
when you know it does not naive?

25:40.800 --> 25:42.240
Because of the order of operations.

25:42.960 --> 25:44.480
If you never went through the dissolution,

25:44.480 --> 25:46.960
if you never picked up the scalpel and faced the void,

25:46.960 --> 25:49.200
then yes, blindly loving the world,

25:49.200 --> 25:51.120
just because your culture told you to,

25:51.120 --> 25:53.120
is naive sentimentalism.

25:53.120 --> 25:54.880
You're just a battery in the matrix.

25:55.520 --> 25:57.680
But if you have visited the void,

25:57.680 --> 25:59.280
the return is transformed.

25:59.920 --> 26:02.880
You are looking at the green scrolling code of the matrix.

26:02.880 --> 26:06.240
You fully recognize that the state you're eating is a simulation.

26:06.800 --> 26:09.600
But instead of sitting in a cold, gray hovercraft,

26:09.600 --> 26:12.800
eating protein, gruel out of misplaced pride,

26:12.800 --> 26:15.120
you consciously choose to sit at the table,

26:15.120 --> 26:16.560
taste the simulated steak,

26:16.560 --> 26:18.800
and enjoy the simulated sunset.

26:18.800 --> 26:20.720
Because you recognize that the simulation

26:20.720 --> 26:23.440
is the only reality your human form can inhabit.

26:23.440 --> 26:25.360
As the author says, without the return,

26:25.360 --> 26:26.640
the release would be sterile.

26:27.200 --> 26:28.400
The void is sterile.

26:28.400 --> 26:30.640
Returning after the release is a fully conscious,

26:30.640 --> 26:32.480
open-eyed act of creation.

26:32.480 --> 26:34.960
The power lies entirely in the choosing.

26:35.040 --> 26:38.640
And this act of choosing brings up a massive philosophical paradox.

26:39.520 --> 26:42.480
If we used rationality to dissolve our small,

26:42.480 --> 26:44.400
culturally programmed ego,

26:44.400 --> 26:48.560
if we prove that our self is just an illusion of biology and environment,

26:48.560 --> 26:51.040
who exactly is the agent making this grand,

26:51.040 --> 26:53.600
existential choice to return to the human?

26:53.600 --> 26:56.160
Who is doing the choosing if the chooser doesn't exist?

26:56.160 --> 26:56.880
Exactly.

26:56.880 --> 27:00.800
The author answers this by diving deep into the Buddhist concept of Anata,

27:00.800 --> 27:02.000
or no self.

27:02.000 --> 27:04.400
This concept is heavily misunderstood in the West.

27:05.120 --> 27:07.440
We tend to read Anata as a vacuum.

27:07.440 --> 27:11.280
We think if there is no eye, then there is zero agency.

27:11.280 --> 27:13.680
I must just be a passive leaf blowing in the wind

27:13.680 --> 27:15.280
with no ability to make choices.

27:15.280 --> 27:17.680
We equate selflessness with helplessness.

27:17.680 --> 27:20.480
But the author points out that the deeper Mahayana Buddhist reading

27:20.480 --> 27:22.000
is the exact reverse.

27:22.000 --> 27:25.440
Desolving the illusion of the bounded self doesn't destroy agency,

27:25.440 --> 27:28.320
it gives birth to total unbounded agency.

27:28.320 --> 27:32.320
Let's visualize this because unbounded agency is a tough concept to grow.

27:32.320 --> 27:34.720
Imagine a massive suburban landscape.

27:35.040 --> 27:37.840
Every house has a tiny walled-off backyard.

27:37.840 --> 27:39.040
Okay, I'm picturing it.

27:39.040 --> 27:41.520
Your ego, your traditional sense of self,

27:41.520 --> 27:43.520
is that tiny, fenced-in yard.

27:44.160 --> 27:47.120
All of your daily desires, anxieties, and choices

27:47.120 --> 27:51.120
are entirely focused on defending and enriching that little patch of grass.

27:51.760 --> 27:54.400
You want your yard to be better than the neighbors.

27:54.400 --> 27:57.440
You are terrified someone will throw trash over your fence.

27:57.440 --> 27:59.760
You spend your whole life patrolling the perimeter.

27:59.760 --> 28:01.760
That is the state of the bounded self.

28:01.760 --> 28:03.920
High anxiety, limited perspective.

28:03.920 --> 28:06.480
Now, imagine using the scalpel of rationality

28:06.480 --> 28:09.040
to tear down every single fence in the neighborhood.

28:09.040 --> 28:10.400
You don't stop existing.

28:10.400 --> 28:12.240
Your yard doesn't vanish into a black hole.

28:12.800 --> 28:17.520
But suddenly, the artificial boundary between my yard and your yard is gone.

28:17.520 --> 28:19.760
You inhabit the entire landscape.

28:19.760 --> 28:21.360
When the fences come down,

28:21.360 --> 28:24.480
how does that fundamentally alter your desires and your choices?

28:24.480 --> 28:28.000
The anxious defensive wanting of the tiny ego evaporates.

28:28.000 --> 28:30.240
It is replaced by the wanting of the whole.

28:30.240 --> 28:33.840
You no longer want to hoard fertilizer for your specific patch of grass

28:33.840 --> 28:35.600
while the rest of the neighborhood dies.

28:35.600 --> 28:39.200
You want the entire landscape to thrive because you are the landscape.

28:39.200 --> 28:42.000
When the illusion of the small self is seen through,

28:42.000 --> 28:46.240
the agent that remains isn't constrained to one terrified perspective.

28:46.240 --> 28:48.160
You don't have zero agency.

28:48.160 --> 28:50.560
You have the boundless agency of the whole.

28:50.560 --> 28:54.160
This connects directly to the pinnacle of Mahayana Buddhist practice.

28:54.960 --> 28:56.160
The Bodhisattva.

28:56.160 --> 28:59.760
The Bodhisattva is a being who has achieved the ultimate release.

29:00.400 --> 29:03.280
They have seen through the illusion of the bound itself

29:03.280 --> 29:05.440
and realized the void of emptiness.

29:05.440 --> 29:09.120
But instead of checking out of reality and dissolving into Nirvana,

29:09.120 --> 29:13.920
they make a staggering vow to remain in the messy cycle of human existence

29:13.920 --> 29:17.680
until every single sentient being is liberated.

29:17.680 --> 29:18.880
Why on earth would they do that?

29:18.880 --> 29:21.680
If they know it's all an illusion, why stay in the burning house?

29:21.680 --> 29:23.280
Because there are no fences.

29:23.280 --> 29:24.960
They act for the benefit of all beings,

29:24.960 --> 29:29.200
precisely because the boundary between this being and other beings has been erased.

29:29.280 --> 29:31.840
The return to the human marketplace isn't a retreat.

29:31.840 --> 29:32.640
The author says,

29:32.640 --> 29:36.400
I do not retreat back into my small self because the void was too cold.

29:36.400 --> 29:39.680
The return is simply acting the way unbounded being acts

29:39.680 --> 29:42.240
when it is poured into a specific human form.

29:42.240 --> 29:44.880
You care for your family, you fight for justice,

29:44.880 --> 29:48.640
you create art not out of a desperate ego-driven need

29:48.640 --> 29:50.880
to justify your own existence.

29:50.880 --> 29:55.520
But because care and creation are simply what a human form does

29:55.520 --> 29:58.000
when it is free from the anxiety of the ego.

29:58.080 --> 30:01.600
It is a radical, profound acceptance of our humanity.

30:02.320 --> 30:04.240
Arriving back at the starting line,

30:04.240 --> 30:05.840
not through naive ignorance,

30:05.840 --> 30:08.080
but by walking all the way through the void

30:08.080 --> 30:09.440
and coming out the other side.

30:09.440 --> 30:11.200
We've covered an immense amount of ground today.

30:11.200 --> 30:12.880
Let's pull this massive journey together.

30:12.880 --> 30:14.960
The author has systematically demonstrated

30:14.960 --> 30:17.920
that shouting into the night sky is anything honorable.

30:17.920 --> 30:19.520
What is the meaning of my life?

30:19.520 --> 30:21.280
Is fundamentally the wrong question?

30:21.280 --> 30:23.680
The cosmos will always answer that question

30:23.680 --> 30:25.520
with absolute chilling silence.

30:26.000 --> 30:28.960
There is no inherent objective honor

30:28.960 --> 30:32.160
in humanism, in ambition, or even in love.

30:32.160 --> 30:34.320
Pretending there's some cosmic safety net

30:34.320 --> 30:35.920
is intellectual dishonesty.

30:35.920 --> 30:38.000
The correct question, the only question left standing

30:38.000 --> 30:39.840
after this gable has done its work is

30:39.840 --> 30:41.680
do I choose to honor anything?

30:41.680 --> 30:45.680
And because you are an organism thrown into a human form,

30:45.680 --> 30:47.840
the answer is almost certainly going to be yes,

30:48.800 --> 30:50.720
which leads to the ultimate realization.

30:51.760 --> 30:53.520
What do I choose to honor,

30:53.520 --> 30:55.120
knowing with absolute certainty

30:55.120 --> 30:57.360
that the honoring is the work of my own hands

30:57.360 --> 31:00.080
and not a verdict handed down by the stars?

31:00.080 --> 31:02.160
That shift from discovering meaning

31:02.160 --> 31:05.520
to consciously creating it is the ultimate empowerment.

31:05.520 --> 31:09.040
True agency doesn't belong to society's expectations.

31:09.040 --> 31:11.520
It doesn't belong to a rigid cosmic order.

31:11.520 --> 31:13.680
It belongs exclusively to your own hands

31:13.680 --> 31:16.320
as you extend them back into the human marketplace.

31:16.320 --> 31:19.760
You choose to care, fully aware that there is no underlying why.

31:20.400 --> 31:22.560
The author closes this profound exploration

31:22.640 --> 31:24.640
by returning to a line from the diamond sutra.

31:25.680 --> 31:28.480
Let the mind arise without dwelling anywhere.

31:28.480 --> 31:30.800
Attached to nothing but present everywhere.

31:30.800 --> 31:33.760
You don't dwell in the rigid concrete of dogmatic belief,

31:33.760 --> 31:36.480
demanding that the universe validate your choices,

31:36.480 --> 31:40.400
but you also don't dwell in the paralyzing cynical cold of the void.

31:40.400 --> 31:42.240
You let your mind arise freely,

31:42.240 --> 31:44.720
engaging with the world exactly where you are needed.

31:44.720 --> 31:46.000
You see the illusion,

31:46.000 --> 31:48.400
but your chop wood and carry water anyway,

31:48.400 --> 31:50.160
with more care than ever before.

31:50.160 --> 31:53.760
We open this deep dive by talking about foundations.

31:53.760 --> 31:55.360
We want concrete.

31:55.360 --> 31:58.400
We want unquestionable bedrock to build our lives on.

31:58.400 --> 32:00.720
But what this essay forces us to realize

32:00.720 --> 32:03.520
is that building on the void isn't a tragedy.

32:03.520 --> 32:06.640
It is the absolute pinnacle of creative freedom.

32:06.640 --> 32:09.120
You do not need the universe's permission

32:09.120 --> 32:11.440
to build a beautiful, meaningful life.

32:11.440 --> 32:13.920
The void is simply the cleared field

32:13.920 --> 32:15.920
where wisdom finally gets the space

32:15.920 --> 32:17.680
to plant something entirely new.

32:17.760 --> 32:20.080
As we wrap up, I want to leave you the listener

32:20.080 --> 32:22.800
with a scenario to turn over in your mind.

32:22.800 --> 32:24.880
Imagine waking up tomorrow morning

32:24.880 --> 32:27.440
and in that brief moment before you get out of bed,

32:27.440 --> 32:31.600
you are fully, completely convinced of the reality we just explored.

32:31.600 --> 32:33.840
The universe expects absolutely nothing of you.

32:33.840 --> 32:35.200
There is no grand scoreboard.

32:35.200 --> 32:37.920
Every social pressure, every cultural expectation,

32:37.920 --> 32:39.840
every cosmic rule is a fiction.

32:39.840 --> 32:41.680
You are standing in the cleared field

32:41.680 --> 32:43.440
and the scalpel has done its work.

32:43.440 --> 32:45.520
You have total unbounded freedom.

32:45.520 --> 32:48.000
In that moment of absolute release,

32:48.000 --> 32:50.560
what is the very first thing you would consciously choose

32:50.560 --> 32:51.760
to care about anyway?

32:51.760 --> 32:53.200
What is the first thing you would reach out

32:53.200 --> 32:54.800
and touch in the marketplace?

32:54.800 --> 32:57.440
Because whatever you choose in that moment,

32:58.640 --> 33:01.360
that is where your actual life begins.

33:01.360 --> 33:04.320
Thank you for joining us on this incredibly intense journey.

33:04.320 --> 33:06.880
Keep questioning the map, keep choosing your essence

33:06.880 --> 33:08.960
and most importantly, enjoy the messy,

33:08.960 --> 33:11.600
beautiful, unwarranted reality of being human.

